WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.110
The fourth Final Destination opens with a
race car accident, so as you can imagine,

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:08.090
the scene is loaded up with references and
easter eggs hidden in the many advertisements

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:09.990
plastered on the cars and around the speedway.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.480
The car that was responsible for the accident
was Car 6.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.049
Each opening disaster in the previous movies
has referenced Hell or the devil.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:20.360
From any almost any given angle you can see
three 6s on the car, representing the Devil’s

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.640
number, and the hellfire decals represent
the fiery inferno.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.490
This car has a number of ads on it, including
Brand X, the same brand of vehicle that would

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.190
eventually take out the mechanic, Andy.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.640
There’s also a Brand X racing poster in
the security guards office.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.480
There are a lot of Xs in this movie.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:41.070
The goth hair stylist, Deedee, has an X tattoo
on her arm, the main character Nick has an XBOX, and the characters Hunt and Janet

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.600
are ex-boyfriend and girlfriend.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.680
The recurring Xs may be an homage to the special
effects company that did most of the effects

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:47.750
on this movie.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.180
Another decal seen on this car and many of
the others is this one, for Heck Race Shifts,

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.130
created by Ryan Heck, the assistant art director.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:57.690
There’s also a banner for Heck Race Shifts
where we see their slogan: “Performance,

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.930
Get it in Gear.”

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:03.120
Nick has a premonition later on where his
girlfriend Lori is grinded up by an escalator

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.120
gear.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.789
There’s also some text on the car’s hood,
“Feel the rush”, which could be a sign

00:01:06.789 --> 00:01:10.369
of the violent stampede out of the stadium
when the crash first occurs.

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:12.859
That’s just the first car in the first scene.

00:01:12.859 --> 00:01:13.752
There is so.

00:01:13.752 --> 00:01:14.270
Much.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:14.770
More.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:19.270
And I’ll be covering it all, so stick around
to the end of this video.

00:01:22.532 --> 00:01:27.340
Directorial duties for The Final Destination
would… for some reason… be turned back

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.299
over to Final Destination 2’s director,
David R Ellis.

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:32.340
And he didn’t really innovate a whole lot.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:37.119
Last time he brought us the Route 23 pile-up
so this time the opener is… another pile-up

00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:38.859
but this time it’s at a race track?

00:01:38.859 --> 00:01:42.859
Aside from that, there’s definitely a miscommunication
about the tone of the franchise between these

00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:43.859
two directors.

00:01:43.859 --> 00:01:47.569
Wong’s movies kind of have some emotional
depth to the characters, while Ellis seems

00:01:47.569 --> 00:01:50.079
entirely focused on goofy over the top kills.

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:51.770
It’s easy to understand why.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:55.109
Wong was promoted to the director’s chair
as a writer, and Ellis’s background was

00:01:55.109 --> 00:01:56.590
as a stunt coordinator.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:00.109
One thing they did get down between the two
of them, was lots and lots of Things You Missed.

00:02:01.109 --> 00:02:04.899
But before we get back into them, this
is my last Raycon ad read of the year, so

00:02:04.899 --> 00:02:07.069
let’s go back over some of the best moments.

00:02:07.069 --> 00:02:10.378
Like when I told you all the reasons you should
pick up Raycons.

00:02:10.378 --> 00:02:14.549
“You'll get six hours of playtime, seamless Bluetooth
pairing, more bass, a more compact design

00:02:14.549 --> 00:02:16.349
that gives you a nice noise isolating fit.”

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:19.659
And that time I told if you if they make good
as a holiday gift or not.

00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:23.180
“They make a great holiday gift cause you
can use them anytime anywhere.”

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:27.330
So many great Raycon memories, and there’s
about to be an even greater one.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.040
Raycon’s being generous for the holidays,
they’re offering all my viewers 15-percent

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:32.069
off right now!

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:37.269
Click the link in the description box or go
to buyraycon.com/czsworld to get 15% off your

00:02:37.269 --> 00:02:39.370
Raycon purchase!

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:47.330
The first thing you’ll hear when you start
watching The Final Destination is the song

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.849
Devour by Shinedown.

00:02:48.849 --> 00:02:53.040
Unlike previous movies, there isn’t a diegetic
Death omen song, that is, one that the characters

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.040
can hear.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.459
But the lyrics are still kind of relevant.

00:02:55.459 --> 00:02:57.910
Like this line: “Smash it and crash it and
thrash it and trash it.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.280
You know they're only toys!”

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.400
There would soon be a lot of smashing and
crashing, and we do see a toy version of the

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.299
main car involved in that crash later on at
the mall, when our characters walk by the

00:03:07.299 --> 00:03:08.340
Megatech booth.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.210
Remember, the race that ended in tragedy was
called the Megatech 300.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.079
And that race was held at McKinley Speedway.

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:18.019
I pointed out the meaning behind that name
and the many locations with the McKinley moniker

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:19.840
in my Final Destination 3 video.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.099
Let’s look at some of the other cars involved
in the race.

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:25.799
Most of these appear on multiple cars or in
multiple places in the stadium, so I’ll

00:03:25.799 --> 00:03:28.139
just be mentioning where I first noticed each
one.

00:03:28.139 --> 00:03:32.520
The red Car 71 has a phone number on it which
contains the number 180, a reference to Flight

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:33.769
180 from the first movie.

00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:36.896
The characters are also sitting in Section
180 in the stands.

00:03:37.539 --> 00:03:40.769
“Tell me again why we chose this over seeing
the movie.”

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:42.480
They do end up seeing the movie later.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.630
This is a clue about another one of the disasters.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:47.959
Car 51 is a red and white vehicle with lots
of stickers.

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:53.200
This one says Hanahan SHT Taylor, representing
executive producer Sheila Hanahan Taylor.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.480
Beck Tires is probably a nod to set designer
Jeffrey Beck.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:59.029
JB Dupuy is named after foreman James Dupuy.

00:03:59.029 --> 00:04:00.029
Hey look.

00:04:00.029 --> 00:04:02.019
It’s me trying to watch this movie.

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:06.549
Car 45six is a grey and black vehicle with
a big sponsorship from Bill Wall Leather.

00:04:06.549 --> 00:04:10.409
This is a clue about the disaster, because
we saw Bill Wall Leather back in Final Destination

00:04:10.409 --> 00:04:14.249
2 on shortly worn shirt of Evan Lewis moments
before his accident.

00:04:14.249 --> 00:04:16.190
Andy is also wearing a Bill Wall shirt.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:20.640
This car also has ads for Casey Moore Gasoline,
named after production secretary Casey Moore,

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.169
Bel Castro Performance Ignition after production
executive Dana Belcastro and Barnard Helmets

00:04:25.169 --> 00:04:27.190
after director’s assistant Kim Barnard.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:29.470
The pit stop guys have patches that say “Wolf
Cams”.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:33.419
As I mentioned in the last episode, wolves
are a Mongolian sign of good luck.

00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:37.660
Car 16 is the bubbly blue car with a sticker
that simply says Colin Grant, that's the name

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:38.699
of the storyboard artist.

00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:43.150
There’s also a Vinitsky Synthetic Oil after
post-production supervisor Jay Vinitsky, Glatter

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:48.400
Tires after supervising production coordinator
Emily Glatter and Lehman something after associate

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.710
producer Tawny Ellis-Lehman. (That's one of the director's daughters.)

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.880
The overall design of this car could 
be our clue about the accident that nearly

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.710
took Janet’s life at the car wash.

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.729
On the back, there’s an ad for Holmberg
Industries, named after production manager

00:04:59.729 --> 00:05:00.729
Erik Holmerg.

00:05:00.729 --> 00:05:02.540
Car 71 says Matthews on the back.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.210
There’s a stuntman named Eddie Matthews.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.480
He’s probably the one driving that car.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.840
“Is it safe to sit here?”

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:09.447
“Of course.

00:05:09.447 --> 00:05:10.290
There’s a fence there.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:11.290
We’re fine.”

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:16.490
*deep breath* I guess it’s kind of a good
thing this line is at the beginning of the

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:19.430
movie, because I still have the stamina to
hear stupid stuff like that.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:21.310
So I’m actually gonna let this one slide.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:25.340
The trailers offer a couple more connections,
like Holman Exhaust, named after art coordinator

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:29.379
Sara Holman, and Dashnaw, named after second
unit director Jeffrey Dashnaw.

00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:32.480
The spilled oil could be a callback to this
part from the first movie.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.860
One of the warning signs is when Nick discovers
they’re actually at a memorial event for

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.020
all the past drivers who had passed away.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:41.610
Like the Flight 180 memorial, most of them
are named after crew members with the exception

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:42.699
of these five.

00:05:42.699 --> 00:05:46.759
In the stands, I noticed a woman with a blue
white and red striped dress, reminiscent of

00:05:46.759 --> 00:05:47.759
the French flag.

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:51.039
We’ve seen this pattern a lot, because the
original Final Destination was supposed to

00:05:51.039 --> 00:05:52.319
be Paris, France.

00:05:52.319 --> 00:05:55.889
Other Paris references in the movie include
this French-style scooter in the cafe, the

00:05:55.889 --> 00:06:00.419
very idea of a cafe is French and also a callback
to the first movie, the picture of the Eiffel

00:06:00.419 --> 00:06:04.669
Tower in Nick’s apartment, this advertisement
at the cleaners is a sideways French Flag,

00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:07.419
Lori has this idea about her and Nick’s
future plans...

00:06:07.419 --> 00:06:09.880
“Lots of traveling like Paris.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:10.880
Maybe some beaches.”

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:14.690
And George gets hit by an ambulance with blue
white and red stripes, just like Terry Chaney

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:16.840
was struck down by the bus in the first movie.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:22.669
He also compares the premonitions to the feeling
of déjà vu, just like Wendy did in FD3.

00:06:22.669 --> 00:06:24.960
“No, no,no, not that. It’s like deja vu.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.914
My wife says that deja vu is God’s way--”

00:06:37.379 --> 00:06:42.229
The name of the cafe that Nick, Lori, Janet
and Hunt like to hang out at is Death by Caffeine,

00:06:42.229 --> 00:06:46.190
and this is the location where three of the
four of them would eventually meet their fate.

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:48.640
Next door there’s a tattoo / psychic parlor.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:52.139
Psychics are often referenced in the franchise,
as the main character of each movie has a

00:06:52.139 --> 00:06:54.370
psychic premonition about a potential danger.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:57.639
The items on the menu are potential warning
signs that Death is coming for them.

00:06:57.639 --> 00:07:03.150
The examples I could see were the Knockin’
on Death’s Door Latte, Deadly Espresso Macchiato, Iced Mocha Fatality,

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:07.960
Iced Vanilla Demise, Frozen Chai Casualties,
Last Exit Truffles (which are maybe a reference

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:11.970
to Final Destination 2’s highway pile-up),
River of Stix Biscuit (a reference to the

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.950
mythological boundary between our world and Hell), Afterlife

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:18.830
Muffins and Iced Fudge Ripple (RIP stands
for Rest In Peace).

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:22.569
The coffee cup logo seen here is also the
same logo seen at The Coffee Beanery from

00:07:22.569 --> 00:07:23.970
Final Destination 1.

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.820
Both locations have someone getting hit by
a large commercial vehicle.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:31.240
There’s also a Hice Pale Ale sticker on
the refrigerator, a callback to movies 2 and

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:34.650
3, where these Hice Pale Ale trucks were involved
in two of the accidents.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:36.820
There was a stunt double named
Cassidy Hice on this one.

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:39.060
I guess stunts run in the Hice family.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:42.470
If you look at the closed captioning on the
TV, you’ll notice the news report is posing

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:45.080
the question, “Are amusement parks going
the way of the dinosaur?”

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:48.780
This is probably a response to the accident
that took place on the roller coaster, Devil’s

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:51.419
Flight, in the previous movie, which took
place 4 years ago.

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:55.170
It’s hard to tell, but it’s possible this
is the same kid who was the ride operator.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:56.949
Maybe he’s here applying for a new job.

00:07:56.949 --> 00:08:00.841
In a later scene, there’s a framed photo,
containing an ominous sign -- the words: Last

00:08:00.841 --> 00:08:01.841
Exit.

00:08:01.841 --> 00:08:04.680
Nick and Lori go to the memorial for the lives
lost at the speedway.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:08.290
There are pictures of some of the victims,
including... apparently the little girl from

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:11.360
the fire meme.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:15.180
This movie continues the tradition of having
something wildly inappropriate happening at

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:16.629
each disaster memorial.

00:08:16.629 --> 00:08:20.539
This time it’s this dude, Carter Daniels,
just dropping an N-bomb out of nowhere.

00:08:20.539 --> 00:08:24.240
At least I thought it was out of nowhere until
I went back and realized he actually has a

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.300
swastika tattoo in the speedway scene.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:27.960
At the memorial, he’s wearing a necklace.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:31.759
It’s actually very similar to Evan Lewis’s
demon-tail necklace in the second one, but

00:08:31.759 --> 00:08:35.440
this one is more of a hook, a clue about his
fate which involves him getting stuck on a

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:37.410
hook and dragged down the street by his truck.

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:39.970
That night, Nick sees some more signs in his
dream.

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:44.060
In the third one Wendy sees the signs in her
photographs, but in this one, Nick just has

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:46.930
more premonitions… which is not that creative….

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:50.340
But one interesting thing is how the words
“It’s Here” are hidden somewhere in

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:51.340
each vision.

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:55.270
In Carter’s it briefly appears in the fire,
in Samantha’s it’s a dark spot on a hair

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:58.500
straightener, in Andy’s it’s obscured
by smoke, in Hunt’s it can be seen in the

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.600
draining pool water, in Janet’s on the inside
of a pipe, in the vision for the movie theater

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:07.750
victims it’s in the fire… again… and
for Nick, Lori and Janet the words “it’s

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:12.120
coming” scratched into the table are now
crossed out because “It’s here”.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:15.880
Another sign from that night is this photo
of Nick and Lori getting stabbed by this blade

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:19.290
that’s just sitting out, which is really
just taken directly from Final Destination

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.720
2, but it’s made a lot less subtle this
time.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:25.640
The first individual death is Carter Daniels,
who’s driving while sipping a can of

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:29.570
Hice Pale Ale, just like the truck driver
when we were first introduced to the iconic

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:30.850
brand of Hice.

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:33.820
What is it about this brand that just attracts
the worst people?

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:37.320
The trouble for him begins when the horseshoe
hanging from his mirror falls and turns on

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:38.320
his radio.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:41.600
I’ve previously talked about how there are
horseshoes in most of these movies as a symbol

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:45.770
of luck, because in Final Destination there
is a big emphasis on luck, chance and fate

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:47.200
all being tied together.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:52.850
Other examples are Hunt’s lucky coin that
he drops before being killed, the die on Carter's locking mechanism, the boss of

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:56.390
the auto shop having a shirt with a horseshoe
and a shooting star, the brand of hairspray

00:09:56.390 --> 00:10:00.390
in the salon is Sprinkle Star, and it shoots
up to dislodge the ceiling fan, the homeless

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:04.580
guy who throws away a lucky penny, and the
movie that Lori and Janet go to is in theater

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:06.380
13 (the unlucky number).

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:08.720
The song that turns on is “Why Can’t We
Be Friends?”

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:12.071
It almost seems like it’s supposed to be
a commentary about how Carter’s end is like

00:10:12.071 --> 00:10:15.670
a punishment for being racist and not being
friends with George, however, if that’s

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:19.260
the case it doesn’t really fit in with the
other deaths in the series because everyone

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:20.260
else was innocent.

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:24.120
I guess the other Carter was kind of a bully,
and a lot of the Final Destination 2 characters

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:27.861
were on drugs, and Frankie is just disrespectful
towards women, but even these don’t have

00:10:27.861 --> 00:10:29.830
any kind of lesson-learned moment like this.

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:33.170
I just find it kind of weird that Death would
take the time to basically tell him he should

00:10:33.170 --> 00:10:37.320
have been better, but then again, he is by
far the worst person in the franchise so far.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:41.180
In every Final Destination episode, I’ve
talked about how Death uses water or liquid

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:42.880
in practically every attack.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:44.760
This movie is maybe the biggest example.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:48.340
The race track explosion started with the
spilled flammable oil, Carter is a victim

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:51.030
of a leaky gas tank, Samantha asks for a glass
of water,

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:52.030
“Oh Deedee?

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:54.390
Could I please get some water?”

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:58.320
...which causes the aerosol can to slide into
the clutches of the hair straightener, the

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:03.110
series of events at the customs shop is set
into motion with this…

00:11:06.737 --> 00:11:11.300
...Janet is nearly drowned in a freak-car wash
accident and Hunt is actually drowned in a

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:12.560
freak swimming pool accident.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:16.101
The cowboy is crushed by an overfilled bathtub
that falls through the ceiling and the movie

00:11:16.101 --> 00:11:19.950
theater accident is a result of more leaked
flammable substance.

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:21.910
That concept is really overused.

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:25.760
The last three characters are taken out in
a beverage shop, and Nick has one of his visions

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:28.480
next to a bus shelter ad for Clear Rivers
Water.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:31.350
Clear Rivers is also the girl from Final Destination
1 and 2.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:34.880
Watch this episode of Horror History for her
full story and analysis.

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:38.220
Speaking of water, I noticed a Wild Surf poster
in Nick’s apartment.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:42.490
Before getting into the film business, director
David R Ellis was a junior pro surfer, so

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:46.500
this may have been a nod to his past life that literally
no one paid attention to.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:50.060
The next sign is when Nick catches a reflection
of his ceiling fan in a mirror on the coffee

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.930
table, a clue about the ceiling fan that nearly
fell on Samantha.

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:56.620
The comb and brush tell us that the accident
occurs at a hair salon and these rocks represent

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:59.430
the rock that the lawnmower accidentally propels
into her skull.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:04.650
I also just have to point out this magazine,
which covers topics like sex, sports, beer,

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:07.290
gadgets, cloths, cars and sex.

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:08.640
And also nuclear sex.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:13.230
The next location would be the hair salon,
which has a hidden connection with Volée

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:14.044
Flight 180.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:27.370
Throughout the Final Destination franchise,
I’ve pointed out several examples of Hell

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:28.730
and demon references.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:33.860
The name of the hair place, Salon Dante, refers
to Dante’s Inferno, the famous 14th Century

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:37.480
Poem describing Dante Alighieri’s 
journey through Hell.

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:41.160
The next victim, Samantha Lane, is with her
two sons after their soccer game.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.920
Their numbers are 20 and 9.

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:47.130
Twenty times nine equals 180, the number of
Alex Browning’s infamous flight.

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:51.170
Across the street, a gardener pours fuel into
his mower -- one of the few fuel cans that

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:53.600
does not get lit on fire in the world of Final
Destination.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:57.660
Based on his hat, it looks like he works for
Jaymes Tools and Parts, a reference to the

00:12:57.660 --> 00:12:59.350
production designer Jaymes Hinkle.

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:02.190
It’s not a Final Destination movie without
our goth girl right?

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:05.580
I also love how the salon has this big old
framed photo of a pair of scissors.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:09.420
As if it were not abundantly clear that they
cut hair in this establishment.

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:13.190
Another ominous sign is seen when the soccer
kids come back with Slushees, the same brand

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:17.330
that Ashley and Ashlyn had before they became
the latest tanning bed victims.

00:13:17.330 --> 00:13:21.250
“I’ve got my eye on you two.”

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:23.520
Hopefully it was the other eye.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:26.850
This incident seems to be an homage to what
happened to Billy Hitchcock in the original

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:31.250
Final Destination, where a piece of scrap
metal shot out from under the train to decapitate

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:32.250
him.

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:33.250
Gotta love this guy’s reaction though.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:34.250
Yep.

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:35.250
Just another day on the job.

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:39.020
Nick soon learns that Death is one step closer
to him when he sees this article.

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:43.750
We find out that Samantha Lane, who the credits
fittingly list as MILF / Samantha, is actually

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:47.580
from Lakeview, which is where the hospital
was located in Final Destination 2.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:51.050
There are many bad omens here: the ads at
the top are all about making your will and

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:54.580
planning your estate, and the related stories
are all about other disasters.

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:59.220
The bus crash on HWY 180 references both the
flight number 180 and the pile-up the following

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:00.880
year which took place on a highway.

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:04.430
There’s also mention of a Ferris Wheel disaster,
and not the first one that’s come up on

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:05.430
Things You Missed either.

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:09.230
It’s probably an homage to the amusement
park crash in Final Destination 3.

00:14:09.230 --> 00:14:14.570
The URL of this article contains a 1/08, which
is also an anagram of 180.

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:16.720
The author of the article is Amy Michelle
Akin.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:20.370
I’m guessing she’s a relative of prop
master Curtis Akin, who also appears as a

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:21.820
golfer at the country club.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:24.390
The article claims the rock was moving at
light speed.

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:26.150
Yeah, I don’t know about that.

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:28.800
It also says the lawnmower guy is named Danny
Wall.

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:29.800
Not Carl.

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:31.520
I guess he was just wearing Carl’s shirt
or something.

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:34.440
Nick and Lori have their friends over to try
to explain what’s going on.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:36.384
Hey, is that a Hice Pale….

00:14:36.384 --> 00:14:37.384
Oh…

00:14:37.384 --> 00:14:41.240
Another article tells the story of another
accident survivor named Pam Smith.

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:44.700
We find out that she was once acquainted with
Brian Gibbons, the farmer's son who blew up

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:46.740
in the closing moments of Final Destination
2.

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:51.200
A police officer on the scene was rushed to
Lakeview Hospital and pronounced dead by Dr.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:54.660
Ellen Kalarjian, the same doctor who resuscitated
Kimbery Corman.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:58.240
Nick and Lori do everything they can to warn
them, but Janet and Hunt are unwilling to

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:00.330
listen to their theories, and head for the
exit.

00:15:01.365 --> 00:15:02.856
“Where are you going?”

00:15:02.856 --> 00:15:04.187
“I’m gonna do what I do best.

00:15:04.187 --> 00:15:05.730
I’m gonna go and get laid.”

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:06.730
Wait!

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:07.730
Take the magazine!

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:12.230
The scene where that happens through, is probably
the most ridiculous scene in the entire franchise.

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:16.880
So they’re in this closed off cabana at
a public pool, with little kids walking around

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:17.880
everywhere.

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:21.310
And there are people sitting like three feet
away, with the only barrier being this fabric,

00:15:21.310 --> 00:15:23.660
which is light enough that it’s just kind
of blowing around in the wind.

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:26.030
And he’s literally yelling to go faster
and stuff.

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:27.830
There is no barrier to sound right here.

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:32.220
But in case, by some miracle, nobody could
hear them doing it, this chick makes it even

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:36.560
more obvious by putting the bikini back on
OUTSIDE OF THE CABANA.

00:15:38.667 --> 00:15:41.290
So moving on, Janet has a pickup from Carver’s
Cleaners.

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:43.360
I think Carver is kind of a slasher reference.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:46.260
She drives to the car wash to try to clean
off her windshield, and if you’re paying

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:49.820
close attention there are several clues about
what’s going to happen to her.

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:51.140
Look at the shape of her car.

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:54.500
It’s very boxy, almost like an aquarium,
and behind the steering wheel there are a

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:55.720
couple of small fish.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:59.110
She drops her quarter into the sewer grate
so she’s unable to fit through to get it.

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:02.561
She suffers a similar predicament when she’s
unable to escape through the opening in the

00:16:02.561 --> 00:16:03.061
sunroof.

00:16:03.061 --> 00:16:06.490
There’s also a rubber duck in front of the
passenger seat, just another clue that this

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:08.110
tub is gonna be filling up.

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:11.740
Anyway, she does survive, but she comes out
of there looking like that Roblox music video.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:15.834
Her car is picked up by Destiny Towing, the
same towing company that Nazi Carter worked

00:16:15.834 --> 00:16:16.334
for.

00:16:16.334 --> 00:16:19.976
That name, by the way, Destiny, indicates
the nature of the Final Destination world

00:16:19.976 --> 00:16:22.370
where everything is part of Death’s design.

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:25.440
Back at the pool this little kid has a super
soaker called the flash flood.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.120
It makes me wonder if this is supposed to
be a reference to the big storm at the end

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:29.120
of the first movie.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:32.660
I already mentioned this when I covered FD2,
but in that movie we see a fish get caught

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:36.070
in the filter at the dentist’s office, which
is a scaled down version of what happens to

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:37.070
Hunt at the swimming pool.

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:40.300
Back at Nick’s house, he and Lori are trying
to make the place safe, and we get a glimpse

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:41.300
of his bookshelf.

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:43.660
The first publication in the stack is Hamlet.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:47.570
This is technically a sign that -- spoiler
alert for Hamlet which came out over 400 years

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:49.390
ago -- everyone dies in the end.

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:52.910
We pretty much knew that though as soon as
we saw that it’s a Final Destination movie,

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:56.630
however, there would be some new surprises
in the Final Destination lore hidden in the

00:16:56.630 --> 00:16:58.352
pages of the newspaper.

00:17:07.669 --> 00:17:11.699
In every Final Destination Things You Missed
episode so far, I’ve explained how each

00:17:11.699 --> 00:17:14.799
character’s name is an homage to an
important person in the horror genre.

00:17:14.799 --> 00:17:18.829
Sadly, in The Final Destination, last names
weren’t really a thing.

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:22.509
But if we do some detective work, using the
original script, it seems that the plan was

00:17:22.509 --> 00:17:23.939
once to continue the tradition.

00:17:23.939 --> 00:17:27.649
I don’t really know most of the filmmakers
being referenced in this one, they aren’t

00:17:27.649 --> 00:17:31.410
exactly household names, but I was able to
find them and confirm their contributions

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:34.590
to the horror genre because I already knew
to look for horror filmmakers thanks to the

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:35.590
other movies.

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:37.559
You can just look them up if you want to know
what they did.

00:17:37.559 --> 00:17:39.280
Nick’s last name was O’Bannon.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:42.350
This was likely a reference to horror writer-director
Dan O’Bannon.

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:44.480
Lori Milligan was named after Andy Milligan.

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:47.340
Janet Cunningham seems to be named after Sean
Cunningham.

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:51.870
Hunt Wynorski has gotta be for Jim Wynorski,
who made a lot of B-movies and horror parodies.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:55.759
I know we’ve seen the wind associated with
Death before in this franchise, but usually

00:17:55.759 --> 00:17:56.960
there’s a window open or something.

00:17:56.960 --> 00:18:00.529
And I like how it just goes from a light wind
towards their faces to this freaking gale

00:18:00.529 --> 00:18:04.379
force in the opposite direction but it’s
just entirely targeted at this full coffee

00:18:04.379 --> 00:18:04.879
cup.

00:18:04.879 --> 00:18:08.070
Like, the paper and the cardboard don’t
move at all, but the porcelain mug just jumps

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.070
off the table.

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:12.690
The spilled coffee stains this newspaper,
causing Nick to believe this is a message,

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:16.220
because only words “Through Action they
were saved” are left dry.

00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:19.190
So he thinks his actions could save George
and the rest of them.

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:22.470
But what he should have been paying attention
to was the contents of this article, about

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:26.259
an out of control truck crashing through a
storefront to kill 3 students from McKinley

00:18:26.259 --> 00:18:27.259
High School.

00:18:27.259 --> 00:18:30.670
This is definitely the sign about Nick, Lori
and Janet’s death in the final scene of

00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:31.670
the movie.

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:34.440
Like many others that we’ve learned about
in this episode, they were taken to Lakeview

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:38.320
Hospital, and like a couple of previous deaths
in the franchise, the driver was under the

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:39.620
influence of alcohol.

00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:43.559
The name of that driver was Jason Koffeman,
a development executive on the movie and the

00:18:43.559 --> 00:18:46.290
article was written by Art Schaefer, a co-producer.

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:49.750
The other articles don’t seem to be related,
but they do have crew reference easter eggs

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:50.799
of their own.

00:18:50.799 --> 00:18:54.450
Thinking they can save George’s life, they
go to his apartment, and just walk inside

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:55.450
uninvited.

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:58.700
They eventually discover he’s been experiencing
the same phenomenon that we saw with Eugene

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:02.350
in the second movie, where he’s just physically
unable to kill himself because it’s not

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:03.350
his turn.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:05.210
“I’ve been trying to kill myself all day.”

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:08.899
But it’s OK; when they tell him they saved
Janet, his attitude completely changes and

00:19:08.899 --> 00:19:10.710
it’s all smiles and popping bottles.

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:14.179
I mean, your best friend still died a horrible
death a couple hours ago, but you’re over

00:19:14.179 --> 00:19:15.223
that by now, right?

00:19:15.223 --> 00:19:18.113
“I’ll get the cookies.”

00:19:18.381 --> 00:19:21.730
After thinking they’ve beaten death, Nick
and Lori make plans to travel, similar to

00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:24.760
Alex and Clear’s idea when they thought
they were in the clear.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:25.760
No pun intended.

00:19:25.760 --> 00:19:27.630
“Are you sure you don’t want to come to
the movie?

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:28.950
It’s in 3D?”

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:30.360
Oooh, so meta.

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:34.470
Nick knows it isn’t over yet when he knocks
over this book, which reveals, yet another

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:35.470
sign.

00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:38.029
I’m not sure why he even has this book to
be honest, if it’s just the same paragraph

00:19:38.029 --> 00:19:39.220
over and over again.

00:19:39.220 --> 00:19:42.860
The Cowboy guy dies, so Nick rushes to try
to save his friends with the help of George,

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:44.070
who also dies.

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:47.950
This movie came out in 2009, so they couldn’t
have possibly known that 2020 would be the

00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:52.120
deadliest year since the Holocaust, making
the address that George died at all the more

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:53.190
mysterious.

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:55.440
Meanwhile his girlfriend is buying shoes.

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:56.440
Wait Lori!

00:19:56.440 --> 00:19:58.909
Don’t forget your pamphlet for 2 free music!

00:19:58.909 --> 00:20:02.309
They head to the movie theater, where many
of the posters seem to be additional signs

00:20:02.309 --> 00:20:03.309
of Death.

00:20:03.309 --> 00:20:05.380
The one on the right here is a parody of Saving
Private Ryan.

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:08.950
The film the girls are going to see is called
Love Lays Dying, which is seen as a significant

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:11.110
sign because Lori is Nick’s love interest.

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:13.690
The female lead in Love Lays Dying is even
named Loretta.

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:15.880
Lori is sometimes used as a nickname for Loretta.

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:19.290
The image on the poster is an eye, and when
Nick sees it on the bus advertisement, we

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:20.460
zoom into the eye.

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:24.130
The major premonitions from the second movie
forward all end with the camera zooming out

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:25.429
of the visionary’s eye.

00:20:25.429 --> 00:20:28.070
One of the other posters is for a movie called
The Death Part Two.

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:31.520
The font and color scheme look similar to
that of The Final Destination.

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:34.789
Remember when I covered Final Destination
2 and pointed out that “Hollywood jumps

00:20:34.789 --> 00:20:36.690
on Flight 180 Bandwagon” headline?

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:40.149
My theory is that this might be the sequel
to Hollywood’s Flight 180 movie.

00:20:40.149 --> 00:20:43.791
There’s also an advertisement on the popcorn
tub for an upcoming movie called ‘Til Death

00:20:43.791 --> 00:20:44.791
Do We Part.

00:20:44.791 --> 00:20:49.120
My favorite poster reference is this one,
for a sci-fi film called The Z Zone.

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:53.659
The poster is a parody of the 90s sci-fi television
series, The X-Files, but the connection goes

00:20:53.659 --> 00:20:58.059
a little deeper than that, because screenwriter
Jeffrey Reddick once created an X-Files Script

00:20:58.059 --> 00:20:59.070
called Flight 180.

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:04.600
It was rejected, and Reddick later adapted
it into a feature film called Final Destination.

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:06.760
The theater chain is called Tagert Theaters.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:11.049
According to something I saw on Final Destination
Wiki, it was named after David R. Ellis’s

00:21:11.049 --> 00:21:12.080
daughter, Tagert.

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:15.830
Inside of the cinema, a series of events unfolds
exactly like the events leading up to the

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:17.759
disaster at McKinley Speedway.

00:21:19.134 --> 00:21:21.284
“Down in front!”

00:21:21.284 --> 00:21:23.037
“Sir, move your feet.”

00:21:23.037 --> 00:21:26.980
Could you imagine a theater employee interrupting
the movie to tell Shane Dawson to move his

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:27.552
feet?

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:28.730
"LEAVE ME ALONE!!"

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:31.500
The third parallel took me a little longer
to figure out.

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:33.652
A man’s ringtone goes off behind Lori.

00:21:33.652 --> 00:21:37.891
🎵 Dixieland Ringtone 🎵

00:21:37.891 --> 00:21:40.177
...to which Lori whispers to herself…

00:21:40.177 --> 00:21:41.177
“Dixieland.”

00:21:41.177 --> 00:21:44.600
After looking it up, I found out that this
was a confederate folk song in America’s

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:45.600
civil war.

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:47.799
America’s civil was a war against slavery.

00:21:47.799 --> 00:21:49.350
Slavery equals racism.

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:53.009
The racist guy from the speedway was sitting
in a similar position in relation to Lori

00:21:53.009 --> 00:21:54.070
as the ringtone guy.

00:21:54.070 --> 00:21:57.524
And if you go back to that scene, he can be
heard whistling the same melody.

00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:07.519
There would be a couple more Final Destination
throwbacks as Nick tries to prevent the disaster

00:22:07.519 --> 00:22:11.960
at Tagert Theaters, including a fire extinguisher
that shorts out like Evan’s in FD2 and him

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:14.820
being attacked by a nail gun like Erin in
FD3.

00:22:14.820 --> 00:22:18.710
The movie theater would ultimately not be
the death of Nick, Lori or Janet, but the

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:24.035
final chronological scene of the series would
offer a few more connections and secrets.

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:37.380
The first sign that danger is afoot as Nick
heads back one last time to the coffee shop,

00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:41.080
Death by Caffeine is the bus that passes by
-- bus number 180.

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:44.559
Then, inside the cafe Janet is wearing a hoodie
with a cherry pattern.

00:22:44.559 --> 00:22:48.300
We previously saw cherries on the necklace
of the hair stylist Deedee, and she was also

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:50.289
involved in a major accident.

00:22:50.289 --> 00:22:54.330
Then the homeless guy who threw away the lucky
penny shows up and starts a conversation,

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:56.009
another omen of bad luck.

00:22:58.312 --> 00:22:59.820
“OK, that was really weird.”

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:01.879
No, there was nothing weird about that.

00:23:01.879 --> 00:23:04.820
I don’t know why you would call out one
of the few normal moments in this movie.

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:08.779
They all sit down together to have a drink,
creating a direct parallel to the final accident

00:23:08.779 --> 00:23:11.830
in the first Final Destination with Alex,
Clear and Carter.

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:15.649
Like the Cafe Miro 81 accident, there’s
an issue with the scaffolding on a nearby

00:23:15.649 --> 00:23:17.029
construction site.

00:23:17.029 --> 00:23:21.080
And like that scene, Nick says essentially
what Clear said but with much more pessimistic

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:21.848
intentions.

00:23:21.848 --> 00:23:24.649
“How do we know this wasn’t exactly what
was meant to happen?

00:23:24.649 --> 00:23:28.463
That out of everyone on Flight 180 you me
and Carter were meant to live?”

00:23:28.463 --> 00:23:33.234
“What if us being here, right now, was the
plan from the beginning?”

00:23:33.234 --> 00:23:36.991
The scaffolds fall into the street, right
inbetween bus 180 and a sign that says 

00:23:36.991 --> 00:23:37.991
Dead End.

00:23:37.991 --> 00:23:40.000
This would be their dead end.

00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:48.120
